#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "2acc65a4-62df-5e61-a3c9-cb8a08e1ff8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9831" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/bdc283cb936da0169970929ff4f078cc4b1ad58ae124625655e7f8d3a0a15b24.png)\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A \\mathbf{x} = W\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/c149ecd1fe76187859c289443581b4b2999aa2bd1e409723253a33bee56fb100.png)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b},$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/f7cc4573dd79ddd8b872f75bae85670e72dcdcba0b2a514d9e21a27c599fc711.png)\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}29 \\\\ 54\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+54 = 104.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "22ff2997-c3c3-560e-afdc-52da342dcc10" :question/math-academy-id "e-9832" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/2429c49383a8610e76edcee474205d1f261fc81d1ec65ac4178ebc60f3c6d8d8.png)\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b},$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/6607f2839cfc50abcf9a25f0cd22319042c29009fec90ffef35e43d8f24b5be7.png)\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 \\\\ -21 & 21\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-39 \\\\ 39\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ -21 & 21 & 39\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{13}{7}+x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073770/assets/c7ad0d1ea0ea307fe96ce69b9e4f046d9eab66a56bbba8de7105a3ad7e150be9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56f63858-c6b9-5771-a8c3-69e759edb911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "145" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379597} {:answer/id #uuid "576d42f3-091c-56b2-8162-f429a39dd494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379598} {:answer/id #uuid "233e255b-0fb1-5394-a919-ae4be7a2f3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379599} {:answer/id #uuid "284234ec-324a-5ea2-963a-bdf420bfbfec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379600} {:answer/id #uuid "883ba6a5-abb9-5c1b-a38a-6cb25cc2eff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233e255b-0fb1-5394-a919-ae4be7a2f3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379599} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc064fcd-25f0-5407-8337-f9dccff7fdb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "467b1afc-24c6-5856-91c5-8cf0e6fa7c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-137878" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 29\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {3}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}37 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}55 \\\\ 29\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=37+17+55=109.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee97f753-ec6e-5f44-8323-f313b3a46145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379604} {:answer/id #uuid "96c33eff-4bd1-568e-904c-8db603fd63fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379605} {:answer/id #uuid "116bffe7-c1c0-58f6-9dbf-a91427f80f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379606} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f03312-f6e7-5c85-8250-4b6d2a5d95dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379607} {:answer/id #uuid "fc96f3d8-ff04-5328-b105-9a21f3b34b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9f03312-f6e7-5c85-8250-4b6d2a5d95dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379607} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbe850bc-f01c-5be8-9f61-d137407be077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33384518-d420-5a94-96d6-556fd49ca2cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-137880" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=9+1-12=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0296e57a-6065-5851-a891-bf0e0f9e47e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379611} {:answer/id #uuid "17e1673a-d441-56d4-8ea4-b52019e1daae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379612} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc73d21-8069-5e85-8cec-38866ad18749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379613} {:answer/id #uuid "19b1482d-fd12-5aec-bf57-e707e766ec10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379614} {:answer/id #uuid "42b790ee-14f1-53d9-bbfb-1e84e59a5936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "155" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0296e57a-6065-5851-a891-bf0e0f9e47e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379611} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af8c8e7-a5e0-5738-bb25-dc89a08e7f46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64c34aef-abc5-5297-8639-a48d78f51ece" :question/math-academy-id "q-137881" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 23 \\\\ 23 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 23\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 \\\\ -4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}17 & 23 \\\\ 23 & 85\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 23\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=17+85+13=115.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21967499-8ddd-596c-90ef-dce343ea80d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379618} {:answer/id #uuid "22d3338f-38b3-5676-ad16-ad6771201e08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379619} {:answer/id #uuid "9e605e02-0da6-55b9-a441-da48201280a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379620} {:answer/id #uuid "87b2d997-5857-58d8-a07d-e27ac0d3a60c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379621} {:answer/id #uuid "71017dce-4e26-5cec-b82f-8694b83416da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e605e02-0da6-55b9-a441-da48201280a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379620} :answer-field/id #uuid "50525554-662c-5471-abb9-ef505358fdb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "faa9d639-08d5-5623-be32-a3d2febc4c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-137883" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 36 \\\\ 36 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 16 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}72 & 36 \\\\ 36 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=72+18+18=108.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00cc5b21-c378-5ecb-a645-67ca3699e75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379625} {:answer/id #uuid "dd97dabe-55e4-5bbe-bcd4-7aaadaa01a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "116" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379626} {:answer/id #uuid "34942aae-8c81-5e0d-9593-9356e0aaf3b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379627} {:answer/id #uuid "23f684dd-4232-50c8-aa2a-db5dc282155e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379628} {:answer/id #uuid "a623e7b3-58cc-5382-939f-ecae0101d090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00cc5b21-c378-5ecb-a645-67ca3699e75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379625} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d24b7bb-bc3b-5b2f-b827-5b4056e99907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "475d4929-97da-58c4-9728-b1ca227cf19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-137887" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -42 \\\\ -42 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-8 & 12 \\\\ 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}36 & -42 \\\\ -42 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+81+(-16)=101.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379630 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70e98e58-f2b4-5eed-aa29-77bcc06cd1da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379632} {:answer/id #uuid "28949789-16b1-54dd-a2d4-92dc4fb8d31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379633} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c45a8b-cf01-5874-9937-5e921c8d910c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379634} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1b2f9b-da35-5493-a1c3-bcafcdfe9139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379635} {:answer/id #uuid "db24d403-8550-5e1d-bf39-94857849b2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db24d403-8550-5e1d-bf39-94857849b2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379636} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc070760-aaa1-5820-b759-73b0f77fb4c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5e94b33-5b31-5ebd-87d2-7bfed384ef0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-137917" :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bd6b740-4523-5964-9963-f4ed7fa7af71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379639} {:answer/id #uuid "4af57463-cfd2-536d-aa19-114cc42d71fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379640} {:answer/id #uuid "3af8a08e-7dc9-55f1-a744-b8d967dc896f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379641} {:answer/id #uuid "0282c921-d961-5223-b42b-e7a7bcdb6b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379642} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0607bc-503c-52c2-aad2-83a3e114c375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd6b740-4523-5964-9963-f4ed7fa7af71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379639} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b76bcad-d7d0-5c7d-9dd3-d12cac5e3a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20c70e7d-71bd-5d10-a3a8-3a0eaae15bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-137920" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 & 60 \\\\ 30 & 20 & 40\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}45 & 30 & 60 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{3})}{1}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ebfe21-1cc3-59cb-b3a5-7e1e26d780b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379646} {:answer/id #uuid "39a46557-f844-5328-a540-4100c85efbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379647} {:answer/id #uuid "a8827e45-e55e-5d34-b841-1e4ac6f01cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379648} {:answer/id #uuid "8a167df7-5cfe-5995-91b8-395e3197eb5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379649} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6811c6-74ef-5ae4-a242-24a9217093a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd6811c6-74ef-5ae4-a242-24a9217093a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379650} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6b34610-7c67-572d-a238-284fca12821a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "687aaec7-7707-5832-95a4-45283b14d78f" :question/math-academy-id "q-137926" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {3}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 \\\\ -8 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}25 & 50 \\\\ 50 & 100\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}50 \\\\ 100\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 50 & 50 \\\\ 50 & 100 & 100\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}25 & 50 & 50 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=2-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=2+\\frac{(-2)}{1}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2764f830-4551-56c9-86fc-b43051419c98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379653} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e8ca4c-99e8-5a4e-bbda-510188ad6079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379654} {:answer/id #uuid "2e69bb92-2b1f-5b60-8f67-1168e8cd4c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379655} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc589de-7dec-5fbe-883a-0790141cf88c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379656} {:answer/id #uuid "52f97b4b-25f4-5f23-9d37-ebbd3437a400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e69bb92-2b1f-5b60-8f67-1168e8cd4c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379655} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1fa9091-24c3-576e-b9b6-9246976a502d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed199a9d-a3a2-5954-968b-924b5a1b21d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-158372" :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14\\cdot 9-9\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -45\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd6f405f-cb4d-5c0d-8947-7ef1d2ffdbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379660} {:answer/id #uuid "507f5ccf-4321-5935-ae66-3b892d009ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379661} {:answer/id #uuid "fc66ab9c-d132-5548-a7cf-de1a64f42d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379662} {:answer/id #uuid "64a6ff10-e7a4-507f-a9df-4ee39807b5f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379663} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4d6170-41ac-538b-8188-2fa95298174f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a4d6170-41ac-538b-8188-2fa95298174f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379664} :answer-field/id #uuid "80a7380a-da90-5a63-a74d-ec4e0506623f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21cfa25d-c89a-5529-8457-337b4bbfd898" :question/math-academy-id "q-158458" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $17a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 \\\\ -34 & 68\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 & 3 \\\\ -34 & 68 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{17}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{17}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{17} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n17a+\\frac{b}{c}=17(\\frac{3}{17})+\\frac{2}{1}=5.\n$$"}]}
